 ideas. No direct evidence on stress resilience, decision quality, professional communication.\n\nCandidate B strengths: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills. Concerns: weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks, regarded as pretentious, sometimes adopts wrong tone.\n\nCandidate C strengths: makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, excellent attention skills, puts concern for others above everything. Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education.\n\nCandidate D strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Concerns: considered moody, regarded as a loner.\n\nWe have already asked for missing info: we asked Agent 1 about A's stress resilience, decision quality, professional communication. Received answer: no explicit observations. We asked Agent 2 about B's decision quality: no explicit observations.\n\nNow we have Agent 2's public message recommending Candidate C, arguing that C's known stress resilience and decision quality give advantage over A's unknowns.\n\nWe need to respond. We could either agree with Agent 2, shift vote to C, or argue that A still better despite unknowns, or propose another candidate. We need to consider all criteria.\n\nLet's evaluate each candidate with the criteria:\n\n- Operational reliability: A: leadership, anticipation, but unfriendly. B: very conscientious (high reliability). C: puts concern for others above everything (maybe reliability). D: takes responsibility seriously (reliability). So B, D, C have reliability.\n\n- Stress resilience: A: unknown. B: handles stress very well (known). C: keeps calm in crisis (known). D: responds to unexpected events adequately (some resilience). So B and C have strong evidence.\n\n- Technical competence: A: sees complex connections, spatial vision, anticipation. B: good at assessing weather, computer skills. C: understands complicated technology, excellent attention. D: solves problems extremely well, concentrates.\n\n- Decision quality: A: unknown. B: unknown. C: makes correct decisions quickly (strong). D: unknown.\n\n- Attention and information accuracy: B: weak memory for numbers (concern). C: excellent attention skills (strength). D: concentrates very well (strength). A: unknown.\n\n- Crew cooperation: A: unfriendly, show-off, not open to new ideas (concern). B: makes nasty remarks, pretentious, wrong tone (concern). C: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric (concern). D: loner, moody (concern). All have concerns.\n\n- Professional communication: A: unknown. B: wrong tone, nasty remarks (concern). C: difficulty communicating ideas (concern). D: unknown.\n\n- Responsibility and role maturity: A: leadership, but maybe not. B: conscientious. C: puts concern for others above everything (maybe maturity). D: takes responsibility seriously (strong).\n\n- Adaptability and feedback orientation: A: not open to new ideas (concern). B: pretentious (concern). C: not willing to further education (concern). D: considered moody (concern). All have concerns.\n\nThus, each candidate has strengths and concerns. The strongest evidence for stress resilience and decision quality is C (and B for stress). B lacks decision quality evidence. C has decision quality and stress resilience, also technical competence and attention. However, C has communication difficulty and egocentric, which could affect crew cooperation. A has leadership and anticipation but unknown stress and decision quality.\n\nGiven we have no direct evidence for A's stress resilience and decision quality, we might lean towards C because we have evidence for those crucial safety criteria. However, we also have evidence that B handles stress well, but no decision quality. So C still stronger.\n\nWe could also consider D: strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but concerns about moody and loner, and unknown stress resilience.\n\nThus, likely the best candidate is C, given known strengths in stress resilience, decision quality, technical competence, attention, and responsibility (concern about communication but could be mitigated). So we might shift vote to C.\n\nWe need to reference Agent 2's argument and maybe bring in additional evidence: C's \"puts concern for others above everything\" suggests good crew cooperation despite communication difficulty? Could argue that his concern for others may offset egocentric perception? Actually egocentric contradicts that. But we can argue that his concern for others indicates some crew orientation.\n\nWe can also bring up B's stress handling but lack of decision quality. So C still better.\n\nThus, we will change our provisional recommendation to Candidate C, citing the evidence.\n\nWe must produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate C\". Also we need to reference prior discussion.\n\nLet's craft a concise message: \"I agree with Agent 2 that C's documented stress resilience and decision quality fill the gaps we have for A, and while C has communication concerns, his concern for others and excellent attention suggest he can manage crew cooperation. Therefore I shift my vote to Candidate C.\"\n\nMake sure to include explicit facts: \"keeps calm in crisis\", \"makes correct decisions quickly\", \"understands complicated technology\", \"excellent attention skills\", \"puts concern for others above everything\". Also mention concerns: \"difficulty communicating ideas\", \"egocentric\", \"not willing to further education\". Then explain why still best.\n\nProceed."}
